n only handle say X tasks a minute or  extracts per whatever period or s X tax  tasks excuse me at once does not get  overloaded with so many tasks that it  buckles under the pressure and if you  need additional sort of fine-tuned logic  on top of this you can use things like  App Engine or cloud functions to give  you that additional consumer logic right  now this fine-tune logic might be that  say pub/sub and cloud tasks don't  support exactly the throttling policies  you want so then you have to implement  them in some sort of service compute  option which because that service can  handle that volume of requests or it  might be that you want to take those  requests you want to perform some  modification to them and only then do  you want to send them to your downstream  service right regardless of which one of  those situations you're and having this  additional layer of service compute can  help you achieve that  so some rules of thumb when it comes to  migrating to service note of course this  is a very very very complex topic if  you're seriously if you or your company  are seriously considering sort of  undertaking this journey what I  recommend is you check out the talk by  my colleagues martin omander  and Adam Ross serverless - OH - from  monolith to microservices they will take  you through this in a lot more detail  than I'm about to now but essentially  what you want to remember first of all  service is not magic right things like  data dependencies things like slow i/o  things like application security these  still exists they are still concerned  service it's helpful but it does not  it's not a panacea it does not solve  everything so the second thing when  you're doing this migration you  generally speaking want to do it slowly  and piece by piece because if things  break it's easier to isolate what the  cause of that breakage was if you kind  of go slow and don't don't rush it  through right now you also don't want to  go all or nothing in terms of old  approaches right like I said sort of  less is more capital e evolutionary than  capital R revolutionary so you want to  take advantage of this you want to take  advantage of the sort of prior art that  exists in this old world that will  enable you to leverage that to build  your new service architecture you don't  want to say reinvent the wheel so to  speak and last but not least when it  comes to making your app totally  serverless this will give you a slight  boost because at that point you won't  have to worry about things like offs  anymore and more or less you'll have a  much more scalable app if if you're  dealing with non scalable components but  the thing to bear in mind is this is not  a reason to migrate your application all  at once the benefit from going totally  service is totally outweighed by the  costs that may be imposed by migrating  your app all at once if anything goes  wrong  so definitely despite this this is the  reason to sort of see it through but  it's not a reason to rush that migration  process though so um now that we've  touched on some of the things that have  changed some of the 0 to 1 changes in  terms of service I wanted to take a  moment to talk about the the capital our  revolutionary aspect of cyrillus right  so these are new patterns that we can  take advantage of that we're not really  available to us before service and  things like functions as the service  showed up so the first of these patterns  is how we do event processing and  automation right so it used to be that  if we wanted lightweight deployments we  had to use some sort of well to be  honest they didn't exist right the best  we could do is use infrastructure which  was kind of had  wait even if we said okay here's this  ten lines of code every time I get a  gmail message run this code right  that would mean setting up a whole  entire application for those ten lines  of code and it just really wasn't worth  it in terms of the time we'd save by  writing all this automation right  nowadays what we can do is increasingly  rely on just individual functions single  functions and we can say hey all I have  to do if I want to run this ten lines of  code is write the ten lines of code and  stick it up on cloud functions right I  don't have to worry about okay I wrote  the 10 lines of code now I have to set  up say you know infrastructure I have to  set up maybe a lamp stack or something  like that another thing I want to touch  on here is the ubiquity of events right  so things like web hooks and pub/sub are  common today but they're becoming  increasingly common in the future right  now web hooks are common with external  platforms what you also see with pub/sub  is that you can access a lot of events  through things like stackdriver logging  right so as we mentioned previously in  that busy wait slide not all operations  will have access to a pub sub  notification when they're complete but a  lot of operations will instead notify  logged a stack driver when that  operation is finished so that's  something where you can say okay well  I'm going to take that stack driver  notification I'm gonna export that  sector logging notification to pub sub  and then pub sub is going to use that to  trigger a cloud function right so  essentially and there's a tutorial on  this on our on our site if you go to  cloud.google.com slash function and  slash Doc's this is a tutorial that  walks you through how to trigger a cloud  function in response to a stack driver  notification so the TLDR here is not  only is web hooks or not only our web  hooks and pubs like becoming increasing  in common but various other services  like stack driver are also starting to  feed into pub sub that can essentially  be converted into pub sub notifications  and of course G suite is no different so  G suite integration is also becoming  easier through things like REST API s  and this type of thing allows you to  take advantage of line of business  automation and making your day-to-day  workflow more efficient using using  various forms of code right for those of  you that have seen any of the like  automation charts out there there's a  particularly famous one from a certain  webcomic  but essentially the way that works is  you have you have a certain amount of  time that you save by doing that  automation right like maybe you run this  task you know X times a day or X times a  week it takes Y amount of time and it  would take you Z amount of time to  automate it right and depending on those  parameters it's either worth it or not  worth it to automate that task now where  this serverless pattern becomes really  useful is if you want to make those  sorts of lightweight G sweet automations  like I said line-of-business automations  you can do that right because serverless  decreases that Z for you it decreases  the amount of time that it would take to  set up an automation for a given task  now rapid prototyping is another thing  that changes a little bit notably a  rapid prototyping and a service world  means that serverless is great for  gluing together disparate api's right so  let's say you have a constellation of  really powerful api's that do really  interesting things then you can quickly  stitch them together using things like  cloud functions and our other service  offerings and use that to create easily  scaled apps right which is great because  if you build something that is you know  the next best thing but you don't know  it yet and you tell your friends and  suddenly you know you wake up tomorrow  morning and it's on you know like I said  it's on a national newspaper or  something because it's the best thing  since sliced bread then you want to make  sure you can handle that scaling right  and that's why a service is great for  that kind of workload now a corollary  that I wanted to touch on real quickly  is also how we do market testing right  so if you want to build something where  you're not sure what the addressable  market for it is service is great  because it will give you first of all a  typically smaller upfront cost than non  service platforms right now this is  Intuit this reduced cost takes place in  two ways the first is the compute cost  is pay-as-you-go so it's very easy to  say okay well if I don't use as much  compute as I think I'll need to then I  don't have to commit to a certain amount  of compute I can just pay as I go if you  underestimate how many users you're  going to get then you can say okay well  I didn't have to pay for 10,000 users  worth of compute I can just pay for you  know whatever 1,000 however many users I  actually get right so you're not on the  hook for that now if you're in that  opposite situation where you think  you're  gets a 10,000 users and you end up  getting a hundred-thousand or you know a  million right the scalability inherent  and serverless  means that apps can handle those sudden  explosions in popularity now of course  you have to design your application in  the right way which is why we're  covering design patterns here but if you  do that then server lists can generally  say oh I understand you told me that we  were gonna get you know a thousand users  but maybe we got 10,000 or 100,000 no  biggie don't worry about I can scale  right so as far as a quick recap of  everything we cover today the first  thing to bear in mind is that service is  more capital e evolutionary than capital  our revolutionary right so many service  patterns are essentially updated  versions of older patterns right and  that being said service does fit neatly  within a lot of these existing patterns  so things like micro service use can  help us increase our separation of  concerns which can make our code easier  to maintain and reuse also these service  backends are uniquely a good fit or a  uniquely good fit excuse me for thick  client front ends right if most of our  business logic is on the front end then  we can take advantage of this to get a  uniquely lightweight back-end and of  course how we persist state has kind of  changed when we're dealing with service  compute we persist that state outside of  compute and of course caching and things  like caching and persisting your data  stores may become increasingly important  now one more slide that I wanted to  recap on so this asynchronous mindset is  of course key and the service  environment right so we're starting to  see things like decomposition take hold  where we take all these tasks we  decompose them and then wrap those  subtasks around using various messaging  strategies such as pub/sub cloud tasks  and HTTP typically you'll see pops up in  cloud tasks HTTP is less common but it  does happen in some cases now of course  the benefits of this approach you get  generally speaking a better throughput  and scaling performance in your  application you also get reduced busy  weight cost if you're dealing with  long-running operations right and as I  said not all of those operations will  conform to that exact pub/sub model  sometimes you have to rely on things  like stack driver or web hooks or  whatever but the same logic applies  now if you're dealing with flow control  because you have to do some throttling  or retry logic or something like that  perhaps you have some sort of non  scalable component somewhere in your  architecture or some retry herbal  function that's where you're going to  want to use something like pub/sub and  if you need that compute you're gonna  use something like App Engine cloud  functions you may not need that compute  in which case you might just use pub/sub  you might just use cloud tasks something  like that then of course service opens  up a bunch of new possibilities like I  said it's it's more evolutionary capital  e evolutionary than capital R  revolutionary but it does have its  capital R Revolutionary aspects right so  first of all how we do lightweight event  processing is increasingly being  modernized through things like functions  as a service and second service enables  us to do rapid prototyping and rapid  scaling at the same time they are no  longer mutually exclusive we don't have  to say we want this to scale rapidly so  we have to set up a bunch of  orchestration and do a bunch of  configuration to get that scalability we  can just build our application more or  less throw it on server less and as long  as we design our app in the right way it  will just automatically scale up and  scale down with whatever workload we  throw at it so that's it for me  if you have any questions please do  reach out to me via email at fast at  Google comm thats the word fast with two  A's or functions as a service my team  and I couldn't figure out what the T  stood for it just sounded nice and then  of course you can hit me up on Twitter  at server loss as well  [Music]

